n":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recompute checksum: ","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":4,"offset":5583},"end":{"line":78,"column":24,"offset":5603}}},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c = Σ((2^w - 1) - mᵢ) → (c₁, c₂, ..., c_l₂)","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":24,"offset":5603},"end":{"line":78,"column":69,"offset":5648}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":4,"offset":5583},"end":{"line":78,"column":69,"offset":5648}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1,"offset":5580},"end":{"line":78,"column":69,"offset":5648}}},{"type":"listItem","spread":false,"checked":null,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For each signature component, hash the remaining times:","position":{"start":{"line":80,"column":4,"offset":5655},"end":{"line":80,"column":59,"offset":5710}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":80,"column":4,"offset":5655},"end":{"line":80,"column":59,"offset":5710}}},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":null,"spread":false,"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":false,"checked":null,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For message parts: verify ","position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":6,"offset":5717},"end":{"line":81,"column":32,"offset":5743}}},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"H^((2^w-1)-mᵢ)(σᵢ) = pkᵢ","position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":32,"offset":5743},"end":{"line":81,"column":58,"offset":5769}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":6,"offset":5717},"end":{"line":81,"column":58,"offset":5769}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":4,"offset":5715},"end":{"line":81,"column":58,"offset":5769}}},{"type":"listItem","spread":false,"checked":null,"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For checksum parts: verify ","position":{"start":{"line":82,"column":6,"offset":5776},"end":{"line":82,"column":33,"offset":5803}}},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"H^((2^w-1)-cⱼ)(σ_(l₁+j)) = pk_(l₁+j)","position":{"start":{"line":82,"column":33,"offset":5803},"end":{"line":82,"column":71,"offset":5841}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":82,"column":6,"offset":5776},"end":{"line":82,"column":71,"offset":5841}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":82,"column":4,"offset":5774},"end":{"line":82,"column":71,"offset":5841}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":4,"offset":5715},"end":{"line":82,"column":71,"offset":5841}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":80,"column":1,"offset":5652},"end":{"line":82,"column":71,"offset":5841}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1,"offset":5514},"end":{"line":82,"column":71,"offset":5841}}},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The verifier \"continues\" each hash chain from where the signer stopped, reaching the known public key endpoint if the signature is valid.","position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":3,"offset":5847},"end":{"line":84,"column":140,"offset":5984}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":3,"offset":5847},"end":{"line":84,"column":140,"offset":5984}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1,"offset":5845},"end":{"line":84,"column":140,"offset":5984}}},{"type":"mdxJsxFlowElement","name":"ArticleSection","attributes":[{"type":"mdxJsxAttribute","name":"name","value":"Security","position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":17,"offset":6004},"end":{"line":86,"column":32,"offset":6019}}},{"type":"mdxJsxAttribute","name":"id","value":"security","position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":33,"offset":6020},"end":{"line":86,"column":46,"offset":6033}}},{"type":"mdxJsxAttribute","name":"level","value":"h2","position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":47,"offset":6034},"end":{"line":86,"column":57,"offset":6044}}}],"children":[],"position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":1,"offset":5988},"end":{"line":86,"column":60,"offset":6047}}},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"One-Time Usage Limitation","position":{"start":{"line":88,"column":5,"offset":6055},"end":{"line":88,"column":30,"offset":6080}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":88,"column":1,"offset":6051},"end":{"line":88,"column":30,"offset":6080}}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Winternitz signatures are One-Time Signatures (OTS): they can only be used safely once per key pair.","position":{"start":{"line":90,"column":1,"offset":6084},"end":{"line":90,"column":101,"offset":6184}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":90,"column":1,"offset":6084},"end":{"line":90,"column":101,"offset":6184}}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The attack works as follows: when you sign multiple messages with the same private key, you reveal intermediate values in your hash chains.","position":{"start":{"line":92,"column":1,"offset":6188},"end":{"line":92,"column":140,"offset":6327}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":92,"column":1,"offset":6188},"end":{"line":92,"column":140,"offset":6327}}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The checksum mechanism requires that increasing some message digits (more hashes) must be balanced by decreasing checksum digits (fewer hashes). With multiple signatures revealing intermediate values at different positions, an attacker can construct forgeries where they use higher intermediate positions to go even higher for message components, while using lower intermediate positions to balance the checksum thus respecting the total hash budget constraint.","position":{"start":{"line":94,"column":1,"offset":6331},"end":{"line":94,"column":462,"offset":6792}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":94,"column":1,"offset":6331},"end":{"line":94,"column":462,"offset":6792}}},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Post-Quantum Security","position":{"start":{"line":96,"column":5,"offset":6800},"end":{"line":96,"column":26,"offset":6821}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":96,"column":1,"offset":6796},"end":{"line":96,"column":26,"offset":6821}}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Winternitz signatures derive their security solely from the preimage resistance of hash functions. Quantum computers cannot efficiently invert cryptographic hash functions like SHA-256 or SHA-3—unlike the polynomial-time quantum algorithms (Shor's algorithm) that break RSA and ECDSA.","position":{"start":{"line":98,"column":1,"offset":6825},"end":{"line":98,"column":285,"offset":7109}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":98,"column":1,"offset":6825},"end":{"line":98,"column":285,"offset":7109}}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using SHA-256 with appropriate parameters provides 128 bits of post-quantum security, making these signatures suitable for long-term cryptographic applications even in a post-quantum world.","position":{"start":{"line":100,"column":1,"offset":7113},"end":{"line":100,"column":190,"offset":7302}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":100,"column":1,"offset":7113},"end":{"line":100,"column":190,"offset":7302}}},{"type":"mdxJsxFlowElement","name":"ArticleSection","attributes":[{"type":"mdxJsxAttribute","name":"name","value":"Winternitz Signatures on Solana","position":{"start":{"line":102,"column":17,"offset":7322},"end":{"line":102,"column":55,"offset":7360}}},{"type":"mdxJsxAttribute","name":"id","value":"winternitz-on-solana","position":{"start":{"line":102,"column":56,"offset":7361},"end":{"line":102,"column":81,"offset":7386}}},{"type":"mdxJsxAttribute","name":"level","value":"h2","position":{"start":{"line":102,"column":82,"offset":7387},"end":{"line":102,"column":92,"offset":7397}}}],"children":[],"position":{"start":{"line":102,"column":1,"offset":7306},"end":{"line":102,"column":95,"offset":7400}}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solana's current cryptographic foundation relies entirely on Ed25519 signatures, which are vulnerable to quantum attacks via Shor's algorithm.","position":{"start":{"line":104,"column":1,"offset":7404},"end":{"line":104,"column":143,"offset":7546}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":104,"column":1,"offset":7404},"end":{"line":104,"column":144,"offset":7547}}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When quantum computers become practical, every wallet, program authority, and transaction on Solana becomes forgeable.","position":{"start":{"line":106,"column":1,"offset":7551},"end":{"line":106,"column":119,"offset":7669}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":106,"column":1,"offset":7551},"end":{"line":106,"column":120,"offset":7670}}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Post-quantum migration isn't something you can do overnight. Systems need to be designed today with quantum resistance in mind, creating hybrid approaches that work in both pre-quantum and post-quantum worlds.","position":{"start":{"line":108,"column":1,"offset":7674},"end":{"line":108,"column":210,"offset":7883}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":108,"column":1,"offset":7674},"end":{"line":108,"column":210,"offset":7883}}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While Solana's base layer remains quantum-vulnerable, developers can build quantum-resistant applications on top of it using Winternitz signatures.","position":{"start":{"line":110,"column":1,"offset":7887},"end":{"line":110,"column":148,"offset":8034}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":110,"column":1,"offset":7887},"end":{"line":110,"column":148,"offset":8034}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1,"offset":0},"end":{"line":111,"column":1,"offset":8036}}},"raw":"import { ArticleSection } from \"../../../../components/ArticleSection/ArticleSection\";\r\n\r\n# Winternitz Signatures on Solana\r\n\r\n![Winternitz Signatures on Solana](/graphics/course-banners/winternitz-signatures-on-solana.png)\r\n\r\nUnlike RSA or ECDSA signatures that rely on mathematical problems like integer factorization or discrete logarithms—, both vulnerable to quantum attacks, Winternitz signatures derive their security solely from the one-way property of cryptographic hash functions.\r\n\r\nThis fundamental difference makes them a cornerstone of post-quantum cryptography.\r\n\r\n<ArticleSection name=\"What is a Winternitz Signature\" id=\"what-is-a-winternitz-signature\" level=\"h2\" />\r\n\r\nWinternitz One-Time Signatures (WOTS) evolved from Leslie Lamport's groundbreaking work in the 1970s. Lamport demonstrated that you could create digital signatures using only a cryptographic hash function, without requiring complex mathematical assumptions.\r\n\r\nLamport's original scheme signed a single bit using two secret values. To sign bit `b`, you reveal the preimage of hash value `H₀` (if b=0) or `H₁` (if b=1). Security relied entirely on the computational infeasibility of finding hash preimages: essentially, working backwards from a hash output to find its input.\r\n\r\nHowever, Lamport signatures were extremely inefficient. Signing an `n-bit` message required `2n` secret values and produced `2n`-value signatures. For a 256-bit message, this meant managing 512 secret values and generating 512-value signatures.\r\n\r\nRobert Winternitz realized in the 1990s that instead of signing individual bits in base-2, you could sign larger \"digits\" in higher bases like base-16 or base-256. This innovation dramatically reduced both key and signature sizes while maintaining the same security guarantees.\r\n\r\n<ArticleSection name=\"Mathematical Foundations\" id=\"mathematical-foundations\" level=\"h2\" />\r\n\r\n### The Winternitz Parameter and Base Representation\r\n\r\nThe crucial parameter in Winternitz signatures is `w`, which determines how we group message bits:\r\n- Base: `b = 2^w`\r\n- Digit range: Each digit represents `w` bits and can have values from `0` to `2^w - 1`\r\n\r\nCommon parameter choices:\r\n- `w = 1`: Each position represents 1 bit (values 0 or 1), equivalent to Lamport signatures in base-2\r\n- `w = 4`: Each position represents 4 bits (values 0-15), base-16 representation  \r\n- `w = 8`: Each position represents 8 bits (values 0-255). base-256 representation\r\n\r\n> Larger `w` values create smaller signatures but require more computation for security. This trade-off is fundamental to understanding WOTS performance characteristics.\r\n\r\n### Hash Chains\r\n\r\nWinternitz signatures use hash chains—sequences of repeated hash applications starting from a secret value: `H⁰(s) = s`, `H¹(s) = H(s)`, `H²(s) = H(H(s))`, ..., `Hⁱ(s) = H(H^(i-1)(s))`\r\n\r\nThe security property is asymmetric: given `Hⁱ(s)`, computing any `Hʲ(s)` where `j > i` is easy (just hash `j-i` more times), but computing any `Hʲ(s)` where `j < i` is computationally infeasible since it would require inverting the hash function.\r\n\r\n### Checksum Mechanism\r\n\r\nThe checksum mechanism prevents a specific type of forgery attack. Without it, an attacker who sees a signature could potentially modify the message to one requiring more hash operations on some chains, then forge those components.\r\n\r\nThe checksum ensures that the total \"hash budget\" across all chains remains constant: if an attacker modifies message digits to larger values (requiring more hashes), the checksum digits automatically become smaller (requiring fewer hashes in other values, which is impossible to forge without the private key).\r\n\r\n### Key Generation Process\r\n\r\nFor a message length of `n` bits with Winternitz parameter `w`:\r\n\r\n1. Calculate signature length: First, figure out how many \"chunks\" you'll need to represent any message. If your messages are 256 bits long and you choose a Winternitz parameter of `w = 4`, you'll need 64 chunks total: `l₁ = ⌈n/w⌉`. Using the formula `l₂ = ⌊log₂(l₁ × (2^w - 1))/w⌋ + 1` we then find that for `w = 4`, where the maximum checksum is 960, `l₂ = 3` and the size of the signature is going to be `l = l₁ + l₂`\r\n\r\n> During key generation, you don't know what the checksum will be because you haven't chosen a message to sign yet, but still you need `l₂` private key components reserved for checksum.\r\n\r\n2. Create random starting points: Generate one random secret value for each hash chain you'll need. These random values become your private key components. Think of each one as the \"starting point\" of a separate hash chain: `sk = (sk₁, sk₂, ..., skₗ)`\r\n\r\n3.  For each private key component, apply the hash function repeatedly until you've hashed it the maximum possible number of times (`2^w - 1`):`pk = (H^(2^w-1)(sk₁), H^(2^w-1)(sk₂), ..., H^(2^w-1)(skₗ))`\r\n\r\n### Signing Process\r\n\r\nTo sign message `M`:\r\n\r\n1. Convert message to base representation: `M → (m₁, m₂, ..., m_l₁)` where each `mᵢ ∈ [0, 2^w - 1]`\r\n\r\n2. Calculate checksum: `c = Σ((2^w - 1) - mᵢ) for i = 1 to l₁` and convert c to base representation: `c → (c₁, c₂, ..., c_l₂)`\r\n\r\n> To calculate the actual checksum value for that message we can use the appropriate checksum private key components and hash them the right number of times based on the checksum digits\r\n\r\n3. Generate signature: Where the base representation indicate the number of times the private key needs to be hashed.\r\n\r\n### Verification Process\r\n\r\nTo verify signature σ on message `M` with public key `pk`:\r\n\r\n1. Recompute message representation: `M → (m₁, m₂, ..., m_l₁)`\r\n\r\n2. Recompute checksum: `c = Σ((2^w - 1) - mᵢ) → (c₁, c₂, ..., c_l₂)`\r\n\r\n3. For each signature component, hash the remaining times:\r\n   - For message parts: verify `H^((2^w-1)-mᵢ)(σᵢ) = pkᵢ`\r\n   - For checksum parts: verify `H^((2^w-1)-cⱼ)(σ_(l₁+j)) = pk_(l₁+j)`\r\n\r\n> The verifier \"continues\" each hash chain from where the signer stopped, reaching the known public key endpoint if the signature is valid.\r\n\r\n<ArticleSection name=\"Security\" id=\"security\" level=\"h2\" />\r\n\r\n### One-Time Usage Limitation\r\n\r\nWinternitz signatures are One-Time Signatures (OTS): they can only be used safely once per key pair.\r\n\r\nThe attack works as follows: when you sign multiple messages with the same private key, you reveal intermediate values in your hash chains.\r\n\r\nThe checksum mechanism requires that increasing some message digits (more hashes) must be balanced by decreasing checksum digits (fewer hashes). With multiple signatures revealing intermediate values at different positions, an attacker can construct forgeries where they use higher intermediate positions to go even higher for message components, while using lower intermediate positions to balance the checksum thus respecting the total hash budget constraint.\r\n\r\n### Post-Quantum Security\r\n\r\nWinternitz signatures derive their security solely from the preimage resistance of hash functions. Quantum computers cannot efficiently invert cryptographic hash functions like SHA-256 or SHA-3—unlike the polynomial-time quantum algorithms (Shor's algorithm) that break RSA and ECDSA.\r\n\r\nUsing SHA-256 with appropriate parameters provides 128 bits of post-quantum security, making these signatures suitable for long-term cryptographic applications even in a post-quantum world.\r\n\r\n<ArticleSection name=\"Winternitz Signatures on Solana\" id=\"winternitz-on-solana\" level=\"h2\" />\r\n\r\nSolana's current cryptographic foundation relies entirely on Ed25519 signatures, which are vulnerable to quantum attacks via Shor's algorithm. \r\n\r\nWhen quantum computers become practical, every wallet, program authority, and transaction on Solana becomes forgeable. \r\n\r\nPost-quantum migration isn't something you can do overnight. Systems need to be designed today with quantum resistance in mind, creating hybrid approaches that work in both pre-quantum and post-quantum worlds.\r\n\r\nWhile Solana's base layer remains quantum-vulnerable, developers can build quantum-resistant applications on top of it using Winternitz signatures.\r\n","highlightedCode":{}}